tric, not willing further education. Candidate A: strengths and concerns as listed. Candidate B: strengths and concerns. Candidate D: strengths and concerns.\n\nWe need to decide which candidate we think is best now. Let's evaluate.\n\nCriteria: reliability, technical competence, calmness under pressure, cooperation, responsibility.\n\nC: reliability 100% (strong). Decision quality quick correct decisions (strong). Positive crew atmosphere (cooperation). Communication difficulty and egocentric (cooperation issues). Not willing further education (adaptability). Overall strong reliability and decision quality, but communication issues could be mitigated? Positive atmosphere suggests some cooperation, but difficulty communicating ideas may be a safety risk. Egocentric may affect crew dynamics.\n\nB: stress handling very well (calmness). Very conscientious (reliability). Good weather assessment (technical). Excellent computer skills (technical). Concerns: nasty remarks (communication), grumpy, uncooperative, weak memory for numbers (attention). So B has strong stress resilience and technical, but communication and attention issues.\n\nD: responds adequately to unexpected events (stress), concentration (attention), solves problems extremely well (technical), takes responsibility seriously (responsibility). Concerns: moody, loner, arrogant, weak leadership (cooperation). So D covers many criteria, but cooperation concerns.\n\nA: anticipates dangerous situations (decision quality), sees complex connections (technical), spatial vision (technical), leadership (cooperation). Concerns: not good at criticism, unorganized (reliability), unfriendly, show-off, not open to new ideas (cooperation, adaptability), eats unhealthily (maybe reliability). No stress resilience evidence.\n\nWe need to weigh. Which candidate best overall? C's reliability is top, but communication issues may be mitigated. B's stress resilience is top, but communication and attention issues. D's responsibility